 2 points in each column using sunny lines. So column n-2 has 3 points; at most 2 can be covered by sunny lines, leaving at least 1 point that must be covered by a non-sunny line. If we don't have a vertical line for column n-2, we need to cover the remaining point(s) using a horizontal or slope -1 line. But those lines can only cover at most one point per column, so they could cover that point. However, we also need to cover the other points in column n-2. Let's think.\n\nWe have n-2 vertical lines. Which columns can we assign vertical lines to? We could assign vertical lines to columns 1,...,n-3 (that's n-3 columns). That's n-3 vertical lines. We need total n lines, so we have 2 sunny lines + (n-3) vertical lines = n-1 lines, leaving one more line to be non-sunny but not vertical (horizontal or slope -1). So we have n-3 vertical lines, 2 sunny lines, and 1 other non-sunny line (horizontal or slope -1). That's total n lines.\n\nNow, which columns are not covered by vertical lines? Columns n-2, n-1, n. That's three columns. We have 2 sunny lines and 1 other non-sunny line to cover all points in those three columns. Let's see if possible.\n\nColumn n (size 1): point (n,1). Could be covered by a sunny line or the other non-sunny line. Column n-1 (size 2): points (n-1,1) and (n-1,2). Column n-2 (size 3): points (n-2,1), (n-2,2), (n-2,3). We have 2 sunny lines, each can cover at most one point per column. So they can cover at most 2 points in each column. The other non-sunny line (horizontal or slope -1) can also cover at most one point per column. So total coverage per column is at most 3 points (2 sunny + 1 other). Column n-2 has 3 points, so we need to cover all three points using exactly those three lines, each covering a distinct point in column n-2. Similarly, column n-1 has 2 points, we need to cover them using at most 2 of the lines (sunny lines or other). Column n has 1 point, covered by any of the lines.\n\nThus we need to assign each of the three lines to cover a distinct point in column n-2. Let's denote the three lines: S1, S2 (sunny), and H (horizontal or slope -1). They must pass through points (n-2,1), (n-2,2), (n-2,3) respectively. So each line must contain one of those points. Additionally, each line must also cover other points in other columns (maybe). Let's see if we can find such lines.\n\nWe need S1 and S2 to be sunny (i.e., not vertical, horizontal, slope -1). H must be non-sunny but not vertical (since we already used vertical lines for other columns). So H could be horizontal (y = constant) or slope -1 (y = -x + c). Let's try to see if we can find such lines that also cover the other points.\n\nWe need to cover points in column n-1: (n-1,1) and (n-1,2). These could be covered by S1, S2, or H. Since each line can intersect column n-1 at at most one point, we need at most two lines to cover both points. So we need at least two of the three lines to intersect column n-1 at the two points. Similarly, column n has point (n,1) must be covered by one of the lines.\n\nThus we need to assign lines such that each of the three points in column n-2 is covered, and also the points in column n-1 and n are covered. Let's try to find a configuration.\n\nLet H be horizontal line y = 1. That would cover (n,1) and (n-1,1) and (n-2,1). That's three points: (n-2,1) in column n-2, (n-1,1) in column n-1, (n,1) in column n. That's good. H is non-sunny (horizontal). Then we need to cover (n-2,2) and (n-2,3) with sunny lines S1 and S2. Also need to cover (n-1,2). So we need S1 to pass through (n-2,2) and maybe (n-1,2) or some other point. S2 passes through (n-2,3) and maybe some other point.\n\nBut S1 must be sunny, i.e., not horizontal, vertical, slope -1. So we can try to find a line through (n-2,2) and (n-1,2). That line is horizontal (y=2), which is not allowed (non-sunny). So S1 cannot cover both (n-2,2) and (n-1,2) because that would be horizontal. So S1 can cover (n-2,2) and maybe some other point in a different column, but not (n-1,2). So we need S2 to cover (n-1,2). But S2 must also pass through (n-2,3). The line through (n-2,3) and (n-1,2) has slope (2-3)/(n-1-(n-2)) = -1/1 = -1, which is slope -1, not allowed (non-sunny). So S2 cannot be sunny if it passes through those two points. So we cannot cover (n-1,2) with a sunny line that also passes through (n-2,3). So maybe we need to assign (n-1,2) to H (horizontal y=2). But H is already y=1. We only have one non-sunny line (horizontal or slope -1). So we cannot cover (n-1,2) with H if H is y=1. Could we choose H to be slope -1 line? Let's try.\n\nSuppose H is slope -1 line y = -x + c. It must pass through one of the points in column n-2: either (n-2,1), (n-2,2), or (n-2,3). Let's try to pick H to pass through (n-2,1). Then c = y + x = (n-2)+1 = n-1. So H: y = -x + (n-1). This line passes through (n-2,1), (n-1,0) (but b must be positive, so not needed), (n, -1) (outside). It also passes through (n-1,? ) Actually for x=n-1, y = - (n-1) + (n-1) = 0, not in T_n. So H does not cover any other points in T_n besides (n-2,1). So H would only cover one point. Then we need to cover (n-1,1), (n-1,2), (n,1) with sunny lines S1 and S2. But we have only two sunny lines, each can cover at most one point per column. So we need to cover three points across columns n-1 and n with only two sunny lines, impossible. So H cannot be slope -1 through (n-2,1). Let's try H through (n-2,2). Then c = (n-2)+2 = n. So H: y = -x + n. This passes through (n-2,2), (n-1,1), (n,0). So H covers (n-2,2) and (n-1,1). That's good. Then we need to cover (n-2,1), (n-2,3), (n-1,2), (n,1) with two sunny lines. Let's see if possible.\n\nWe have S1 and S2 sunny. They can each cover at most one point per column. So we need to cover points in column n-2: (n-2,1) and (n-2,3) (two points). So S1 could cover (n-2,1), S2 cover (n-2,3). Then we need to cover (n-1,2) and (n,1). Each of S1 and S2 can also intersect column n-1 and n at one point each. So we could assign S1 to also cover (n-1,2) (line through (n-2,1) and (n-1,2) has slope (2-1)/(n-1-(n-2)) = 1/1 = 1, which is sunny (slope 1 is not 0, infinite, -1). So S1: line through (n-2,1) and (n-1,2) has slope 1, equation y - 1 = 1*(x - (n-2)) => y = x - (n-2) + 1 = x - n + 3? Actually compute: y = x - (n-2) + 1 = x - n + 3. Let's check at x=n-2: y = (n-2) - n + 3 = 1, correct. At x=n-1: y = (n-1) - n + 3 = 2, correct. So S1 is sunny (slope 1). Good.\n\nNow S2 must cover (n-2,3) and (n,1). The line through (n-2,3) and (n,1) has slope (1-3)/(n-(n-2)) = -2/2 = -1. That's slope -1, which is not sunny. So S2 cannot be sunny if it passes through those two points. Could S2 cover (n-2,3) and (n-1,2) maybe? But (n-1,2) is already covered by S1. Could S2 cover (n-2,3) and (n,1) via a different line? No, line is unique. So S2 cannot be sunny and cover (n,1) if it also covers (n-2,3). So maybe we assign (n,1) to H? But H is slope -1 line y = -x + n, which at x=n gives y = -n + n = 0, not (n,1). So H does not cover (n,1). So we need to cover (n,1) with a sunny line. But we have only S2 left, which cannot be sunny if it also covers (n-2,3). So maybe we assign (n-2,3) to H? But H already covers (n-2,2) and (n-1,1). H cannot also cover (n-2,3) because slope -1 line cannot pass through two points with same x coordinate. So not possible.\n\nThus this assignment fails.\n\nLet's try H being horizontal y=2. That covers (n-2,2), (n-1,2), (n,2) (but (n,2) is not in T_n because n+2 > n+1). Actually (n,2) has a+b = n+2 > n+1, so not in T_n. So H covers (n-2,2) and (n-1,2). That's good. Then we need to cover (n-2,1), (n-2,3), (n-1,1), (n,1) with two sunny lines. Let's see if possible.\n\nWe have S1 and S2 sunny. They can each cover at most one point per column. So we need to cover column n-2: points (n-2,1) and (n-2,3) (two points). So S1 could cover (n-2,1), S2 cover (n-2,3). Then we need to cover column n-1: point (n-1,1) (since (n-1,2) is covered by H). And column n: point (n,1). So we need S1 and S2 to also cover those points. S1 already passes through (n-2,1). Could we choose S1 to also pass through (n-1,1)? That would be horizontal line y=1, not sunny. So S1 cannot cover (n-1,1). Could S1 cover (n,1) as well? That would be horizontal line y=1 again. So not possible. So S1 cannot cover any other point in column n-1 or n. S2 passes through (n-2,3). Could S2 also pass through (n-1,1) or (n,1)? Let's compute slopes.\n\nLine through (n-2,3) and (n-1,1) has slope (1-3)/(n-1-(n-2)) = -2/1 = -2, which is sunny (since slope -2 is not 0, infinite, -1). So S2 could be line with slope -2 passing through (n-2,3) and (n-1,1). That line also passes through (n,? ) Let's compute: equation: y - 3 = -2 (x - (n-2)). So y = -2x + 2(n-2) + 3 = -2x + 2n - 4 + 3 = -2x + 2n - 1. At x=n, y = -2n + 2n - 1 = -1, not (n,1). So S2 does not cover (n,1). So we still need to cover (n,1). Could S1 cover (n,1)? S1 passes through (n-2,1). The line through (n-2,1) and (n,1) is horizontal y=1, not sunny. So S1 cannot be sunny. So we cannot cover (n,1) with any sunny line if we already used S1 for (n-2,1) and S2 for (n-2,3) and (n-1,1). So we need a third sunny line or a non-sunny line to cover (n,1). But we only have 2 sunny lines. So impossible.\n\nThus this assignment fails.\n\nThus seems impossible to cover all points with exactly 2 sunny lines. The solution's argument is plausible.\n\nNow, what about k >= 4? The solution claims impossible because each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, so with k >= 4 we need at least 4 columns to have at least one sunny line covering a point. But we have only n lines total, and we need vertical lines for columns with many points. The solution's argument is a bit vague but likely correct. Let's try to see if k=4 could be possible for some n.\n\nWe need to cover all points with n lines, exactly 4 sunny lines. The rest n-4 lines are non-sunny (vertical/horizontal/slope -1). Since vertical lines are the only ones that can cover multiple points in a column, we need to allocate vertical lines to columns that have many points. The three rightmost columns have sizes 3,2,1. The solution says they force a vertical line for column n-2 (size 3). Let's examine that claim.\n\nIf we have k sunny lines, each can cover at most one point per column. So column n-2 has 3 points. If k <= 2, then at most 2 points can be covered by sunny lines, leaving at least 1 point that must be covered by a non-sunny line. If we don't have a vertical line for column n-2, we need to cover that point with a horizontal or slope -1 line. But those lines can only cover one point per column, so they could cover that point. So maybe we don't need a vertical line for column n-2. However, the solution's argument for k <= 2 says we need a vertical line for column n-2. Let's examine more carefully.\n\nIf we have k=2, we have 2 sunny lines. They can each cover at most one point in column n-2, covering at most 2 of the 3 points. The remaining point must be covered by a non-sunny line. If we use a horizontal line, it can cover at most one point in column n-2, which is fine. So we could have a horizontal line covering the remaining point. So we don't necessarily need a vertical line for column n-2. However, the solution later uses the fact that the point (n,1) cannot be covered by a sunny line together with a point of column n-1, forcing a third sunny line. Let's examine that.\n\nThe solution's argument for k <= 2: \"If k <= 2 then column x=n-2 (which contains three points) cannot be covered solely by sunny lines; consequently one of the non-sunny lines must be vertical and must be the line x=n-2.\" This is not necessarily true: the remaining point could be covered by a horizontal line or slope -1 line, not necessarily vertical. So the claim that a vertical line must be used is questionable. However, they later use that vertical line to argue about column n-1 and n. But perhaps there is a different arrangement that avoids vertical line for column n-2 and still uses only 2 sunny lines? Let's try to construct a configuration with k=2.\n\nWe need n lines total. Let's try to use vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-3 (n-3 vertical lines). Then we have 2 sunny lines and 1 other non-sunny line (horizontal or slope -1). That's total n lines. As before, we need to cover columns n-2, n-1, n with these three lines. Let's try to find a configuration that works.\n\nWe need to cover points in column n-2: (n-2,1), (n-2,2), (n-2,3). We have 2 sunny lines and 1 non-sunny line. The non-sunny line could be horizontal y=1, covering (n-2,1) and also (n-1,1) and (n,1). Then we need to cover (n-2,2) and (n-2,3) with the two sunny lines. Also need to cover (n-1,2). Let's see if we can assign S1 to cover (n-2,2) and (n-1,2) (line through those points is horizontal y=2, not sunny). So not possible. Could S1 cover (n-2,2) and (n,1)? That line has slope (1-2)/(n-(n-2)) = -1/2, which is sunny (since slope -0.5 is not 0, infinite, -1). So S1 could be line through (n-2,2) and (n,1). That line also passes through (n-1,? ) Let's compute: equation: slope m = -1/2. Using point (n,1): y - 1 = -1/2 (x - n). So y = -1/2 x + n/2 + 1. At x=n-1: y = -1/2 (n-1) + n/2 + 1 = (-n/2 + 1/2) + n/2 + 1 = 1/2 + 1 = 1.5? Actually compute: -1/2 (n-1) = -n/2 + 1/2. Then + n/2 + 1 = (-n/2 + 1/2) + n/2 + 1 = 1/2 + 1 = 3/2 = 1.5. Not integer, not a point in T_n. So S1 does not cover any point in column n-1. That's okay.\n\nNow we need to cover (n-2,3) and (n-1,2) with S2. The line through (n-2,3) and (n-1,2) has slope (2-3)/(n-1-(n-2)) = -1/1 = -1, which is not sunny. So S2 cannot be sunny if it covers both those points. Could S2 cover (n-2,3) and (n,1)? That line has slope (1-3)/(n-(n-2)) = -2/2 = -1, not sunny. So not possible. Could S2 cover (n-2,3) and (n-1,1)? That line has slope (1-3)/(n-1-(n-2)) = -2/1 = -2, which is sunny. So S2 could be line through (n-2,3) and (n-1,1). But (n-1,1) is already covered by H (horizontal y=1). That's okay; a point can be covered by multiple lines, but we need each point to be covered by at least one line. So S2 covering (n-1,1) is fine. However, we still need to cover (n-1,2). That point is not yet covered: H covers (n-1,1) but not (n-1,2). S1 does not cover (n-1,2). S2 does not cover (n-1,2). So (n-1,2) remains uncovered. So this configuration fails.\n\nMaybe we can choose H to be horizontal y=2, covering (n-2,2) and (n-1,2). Then we need to cover (n-2,1), (n-2,3), (n-1,1), (n,1). Use S1 to cover (n-2,1) and (n,1) (horizontal y=1 not allowed). So S1 cannot be sunny if covering those two points. Could S1 cover (n-2,1) and (n-1,1)? That's horizontal y=1, not sunny. So not possible. Could S1 cover (n-2,1) and (n-1,2)? That's line slope (2-1)/(n-1-(n-2)) = 1, sunny. So S1 could be line through (n-2,1) and (n-1,2). That line also passes through (n,? ) Let's compute: slope 1, equation y - 1 = 1*(x - (n-2)) => y = x - n + 3. At x=n: y = n - n + 3 = 3. So S1 passes through (n,3), which is not in T_n because n+3 > n+1 for n>=3. So S1 only covers those two points. Good.\n\nNow we need to cover (n-2,3) and (n,1). Use S2 to cover (n-2,3) and (n,1). Slope = (1-3)/(n-(n-2)) = -2/2 = -1, not sunny. So not allowed. Could S2 cover (n-2,3) and (n-1,1)? That's slope (1-3)/(n-1-(n-2)) = -2/1 = -2, sunny. So S2 could be line through (n-2,3) and (n-1,1). That line also passes through (n,? ) Let's compute: slope -2, equation y - 3 = -2 (x - (n-2)) => y = -2x + 2(n-2) + 3 = -2x + 2n - 4 + 3 = -2x + 2n - 1. At x=n: y = -2n + 2n - 1 = -1, not (n,1). So (n,1) remains uncovered. We need to cover (n,1). Could H be vertical line x=n? But H is horizontal y=2. So not covered. So we need a vertical line for column n, but we have no vertical lines left (we used n-3 vertical lines for columns 1..n-3). So we need to adjust.\n\nThus seems impossible to cover all points with exactly 2 sunny lines. The solution's argument may be correct, but we need to verify thoroughly.\n\nNow, what about k >= 4? Let's try to see if k=4 could be possible for some n. Suppose we have 4 sunny lines, and n-4 non-sunny lines. We can use vertical lines for many columns. The three rightmost columns have sizes 3,2,1. With 4 sunny lines, we can cover up to 4 points in each column, but each column only has at most 3 points. So we could cover all points in those columns using sunny lines only, without vertical lines. However, we need to cover all points in all columns. Let's think about the general constraints.\n\nWe have n columns. Each sunny line can cover at most one point per column. So with k sunny lines, we can cover at most k points in each column. For column c with h_c points, we need to cover the remaining h_c - k points (if positive) using non-sunny lines. Non-sunny lines that are not vertical can also cover at most one point per column. So to cover the remaining points, we need vertical lines for columns where h_c - k > number of non-vertical non-sunny lines that can be assigned to that column. But we have n - k non-sunny lines total. Among them, we can have some vertical lines, some horizontal, some slope -1. Horizontal and slope -1 lines can each cover at most one point per column, but they can be used across many columns (each line can intersect many columns, covering one point per column). So we could use a single horizontal line to cover one point in each column (if the y coordinate matches a point in each column). However, the points in each column have different y coordinates, so a single horizontal line cannot cover a point in each column unless the y coordinate is the same across columns, which is not the case because column c has points (c,b) for b from 1 to n+1-c. So the set of possible y values across columns is not uniform. However, we could use multiple horizontal lines, each covering a set of points across columns. Similarly for slope -1 lines.\n\nBut the main difficulty is that we have only n lines total, and we need to cover all points. The solution's argument for k >= 4 is that we would need at least 4 columns to rely on sunny lines for at least one point each, leaving at most n-4 vertical lines to cover the rest, but the three rightmost columns already force a vertical line for column n-2, so we would run out of vertical lines before covering all columns. This argument seems not rigorous but plausible.\n\nLet's try to construct a configuration with k=4 for some n, maybe n=4 or n=5. Let's test small n manually.\n\nn=3: T_3 = points (a,b) with a,b positive integers and a+b <= 4. So points: (1,1), (1,2), (2,1), (1,3), (2,2), (3,1). That's 6 points. We need 3 lines total (n=3). We need exactly k sunny lines.\n\nWe can try to find a configuration with k=4? Not possible because we only have 3 lines. So k cannot exceed n. So for n=3, k can be at most 3. So k=4 not relevant.\n\nn=4: T_4 points with a+b <=5. Points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4); (2,1),(2,2),(2,3); (3,1),(3,2); (4,1). That's total 10 points. Need 4 lines. Could we have k=4? That would mean all 4 lines are sunny. Each sunny line can cover at most one point per column. There are 4 columns (x=1..4). So each column can have at most 4 points covered by sunny lines (since each line can cover at most one point per column). Column 1 has 4 points, column 2 has 3 points, column 3 has 2 points, column 4 has 1 point. So we could theoretically cover all points with 4 sunny lines if each line picks a distinct point in each column, covering all points. But each line can only have one point per column, but can have multiple points across columns. So we need to assign each point to some line such that each line picks at most one point per column. This is like a matching problem: we need to partition the set of points into at most 4 lines, each line being a straight line (sunny). The geometry constraints are strong.\n\nLet's try to see if we can cover all points with 4 sunny lines for n=4. The solution says only k=0,1,3 possible. So k=4 should be impossible. Let's try to see if we can find a counterexample.\n\nWe need 4 distinct sunny lines covering all 10 points. Let's try to see if we can use lines with slopes that allow covering many points. For example, line with slope -1 passes through points (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1). That's a line of slope -1, which is not sunny (since slope -1 is excluded). So we cannot use that line. Horizontal lines are not sunny. Vertical lines are not sunny. So we need lines with slopes other than 0, infinite, -1.\n\nWe could try lines with slope 1: line y = x + c. Which points satisfy y = x + c? For integer points (a,b) with a+b <=5, we have b = a + c => a + (a + c) <=5 => 2a + c <=5 => a <= (5 - c)/2. For each c, we can get some points. For c = 0, line y = x passes through (1,1),(2,2). (3,3) not in T_4 because 3+3=6 >5. So line y=x covers (1,1),(2,2). That's two points. For c = -1, line y = x - 1 passes through (2,1),(3,2),(4,3) but (4,3) not in T_4 because 4+3=7 >5. So line y = x - 1 covers (2,1),(3,2). For c = 1, line y = x + 1 passes through (1,2),(2,3),(3,4) but (3,4) not in T_4 because 3+4=7 >5. So line y = x + 1 covers (1,2),(2,3). For c = 2, line y = x + 2 passes through (1,3),(2,4) but (2,4) not in T_4 because 2+4=6 >5. So line y = x + 2 covers (1,3). For c = 3, line y = x + 3 passes through (1,4) only. So slope 1 lines can cover at most 2 points each (except maybe one point). So we could use slope 1 lines to cover many points.\n\nWe also have lines with other slopes, e.g., slope 2, -2, 1/2, -1/2, etc. Let's see if we can cover all points with 4 sunny lines.\n\nWe need to cover column 1 points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4). Since each sunny line can cover at most one point in column 1, we need at least 4 lines to cover all 4 points in column 1. So we need exactly 4 lines, each covering a distinct point in column 1. So each line must pass through a distinct point in column 1. So we need to assign each of the 4 lines to one of the points (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4). Then each line can also cover other points in other columns.\n\nThus we need to find 4 lines, each passing through a distinct point in column 1, and collectively covering all points in columns 2,3,4.\n\nLet's denote lines L1 through (1,1), L2 through (1,2), L3 through (1,3), L4 through (1,4). They must be distinct lines (different slopes). They cannot be vertical (x=1) because vertical is non-sunny. So they must have finite slopes not equal to 0 or -1. So each line is of form y = m_i (x - 1) + y_i where y_i is the y-coordinate of the point in column 1.\n\nThus L1: passes through (1,1) => equation y = m1 (x - 1) + 1. L2: y = m2 (x - 1) + 2. L3: y = m3 (x - 1) + 3. L4: y = m4 (x - 1) + 4.\n\nWe need to cover points in column 2: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3). Each line can intersect column 2 at at most one point. So we need to assign each of these three points to some line. Since we have 4 lines, we can assign each point to a distinct line, leaving one line not covering any point in column 2 (or covering a point already covered). Similarly for column 3: points (3,1),(3,2). Column 4: point (4,1). So we need to assign each point to some line.\n\nWe need to find slopes m_i such that each line passes through its designated point in column 1 and also passes through some other points in other columns. This is a combinatorial design problem.\n\nLet's try to assign:\n\n- L1 (through (1,1)) also passes through (2,2) maybe? That would require slope m1 = (2-1)/(2-1) = 1. So L1: y = (x-1) + 1 = x. Indeed passes through (1,1),(2,2). It also passes through (3,3) but not in T_4. So L1 covers (1,1) and (2,2). Good.\n\n- L2 (through (1,2)) also passes through (2,3)? Slope = (3-2)/(2-1) = 1. So L2 would be same slope as L1, but then L2 would be line y = (x-1) + 2 = x+1. That passes through (1,2),(2,3). That's okay. So L2 slope 1 as well. But then L1 and L2 are distinct lines (different intercept). That's allowed. So L2 covers (1,2) and (2,3). Good.\n\n- L3 (through (1,3)) could pass through (2,1)? Slope = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2. So L3: y = -2 (x-1) + 3 = -2x + 5. At x=2, y = -4 + 5 = 1, correct. At x=3, y = -6 + 5 = -1, not in T_4. So L3 covers (1,3) and (2,1). Good.\n\n- L4 (through (1,4)) could pass through (3,1)? Let's see slope = (1-4)/(3-1) = -3/2 = -1.5, which is not -1, not 0, not infinite, so it's sunny. So L4: y = -1.5 (x-1) + 4 = -1.5x + 1.5 + 4 = -1.5x + 5.5. At x=2, y = -3 + 5.5 = 2.5, not integer. At x=3, y = -4.5 + 5.5 = 1, correct. So L4 passes through (1,4) and (3,1). Good.\n\nNow we need to cover remaining points: (3,2) and (4,1). Let's see which lines cover them.\n\n- L1: y = x, at x=3 gives y=3, not (3,2). At x=4 gives y=4, not (4,1). So L1 does not cover those.\n\n- L2: y = x+1, at x=3 gives y=4, not (3,2). At x=4 gives y=5, not (4,1). So no.\n\n- L3: y = -2x + 5, at x=3 gives y = -6 + 5 = -1, not (3,2). At x=4 gives y = -8 + 5 = -3, not (4,1). So no.\n\n- L4: y = -1.5x + 5.5, at x=3 gives y=1 (we already have (3,1)). At x=4 gives y = -6 + 5.5 = -0.5, not (4,1). So none cover (3,2) or (4,1). So we need to adjust.\n\nWe could try to assign (3,2) to some line. For example, L1 could be changed to pass through (3,2) instead of (2,2). But L1 already passes through (1,1). To also pass through (3,2), slope = (2-1)/(3-1) = 1/2. So L1: y = 0.5 (x-1) + 1 = 0.5x + 0.5. At x=2, y = 1.5, not integer. So L1 would not cover any point in column 2. That's okay, but we need to cover (2,2) maybe by another line. Let's try to systematically search for a configuration.\n\nBut this is a small case; we can try to see if any configuration exists with 4 sunny lines covering all points. This is a combinatorial geometry problem. It might be impossible due to constraints. The solution claims only k=0,1,3 possible for all n >= 3. Let's test for n=4 if k=3 is possible (they gave a construction). For n=4, k=3: they propose three sunny lines covering points (4,1),(3,1),(2,1),(3,2),(2,2),(2,3) and vertical lines for columns 1 (x=1). Let's check: For n=4, columns: x=1 has points (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4). They propose vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-3 = 1 (since n-3 = 1). So vertical line x=1 covers all points in column 1. Then three sunny lines cover the six points listed: (4,1),(3,1),(2,1),(3,2),(2,2),(2,3). Indeed those are all points in columns 2,3,4. Let's verify the lines they gave:\n\nL1: y-1 = -1/2 (x-4). This line passes through (4,1) and (2,2)? Let's check: For x=2, RHS = -1/2 (2-4) = -1/2 * (-2) = 1. So y-1 = 1 => y=2. So (2,2) is on L1. Also passes through (3,? ) For x=3, RHS = -1/2 (3-4) = -1/2 * (-1) = 0.5, so y = 1.5, not integer. So L1 covers (4,1) and (2,2). Good.\n\nL2: y-1 = -2 (x-(3)). For x=3, RHS = -2*0 = 0 => y=1 => (3,1). For x=2, RHS = -2*(2-3) = -2*(-1) = 2 => y=3 => (2,3). So L2 covers (3,1) and (2,3). Good.\n\nL3: y-1 = (x-(2)). For x=2, RHS = 0 => y=1 => (2,1). For x=3, RHS = 1 => y=2 => (3,2). So L3 covers (2,1) and (3,2). So indeed the three sunny lines cover the six points: (4,1) from L1, (2,2) from L1, (3,1) from L2, (2,3) from L2, (2,1) from L3, (3,2) from L3. So all points in columns 2,3,4 are covered. So k=3 works for n=4.\n\nNow, is k=2 possible for n=4? Let's try to see if we can find a configuration with exactly 2 sunny lines and 2 non-sunny lines (vertical/horizontal/slope -1). We need to cover all points. Let's try to see if we can find a configuration.\n\nWe have 4 columns. Column 1 has 4 points. With only 2 sunny lines, we can cover at most 2 points in column 1 using sunny lines. The remaining 2 points must be covered by non-sunny lines. Non-sunny lines can be vertical (cover all points in a column) or horizontal (cover one point per column) or slope -1 (cover one point per column). Since we have only 2 non-sunny lines, we need to cover the remaining 2 points in column 1. If we use a vertical line x=1, that covers all 4 points in column 1, but then we would have used one non-sunny line for column 1, leaving only one non-sunny line for other columns. That might be insufficient. Let's try to see.\n\nOption 1: Use vertical line x=1 (non-sunny). Then column 1 is fully covered. We have 1 vertical line used, leaving 1 non-sunny line left (horizontal or slope -1). We have 2 sunny lines left. So total lines: vertical x=1, one other non-sunny line (maybe horizontal y=2?), and two sunny lines. Need to cover columns 2,3,4.\n\nColumn 2 has points (2,1),(2,2),(2,3). We have 2 sunny lines, each can cover at most one point in column 2, so they can cover at most 2 points. The remaining point must be covered by the remaining non-sunny line (horizontal or slope -1). So we need to choose a non-sunny line that passes through one of the points in column 2. Let's try horizontal y=2: passes through (2,2). Then we need to cover (2,1) and (2,3) with sunny lines. That's possible: we can have sunny line S1 passing through (2,1) and maybe other points, and S2 passing through (2,3). Then we need to cover column 3 points (3,1),(3,2). Each sunny line can cover at most one point in column 3. So we need to assign them accordingly. Let's try to assign S1 to cover (2,1) and (3,2) maybe? S1 passes through (2,1) and (3,2) => slope = (2-1)/(3-2) = 1 => line y = x - 1? Actually line through (2,1) and (3,2) is y = x - 1. That's slope 1, sunny. It also passes through (4,3) which is not in T_4. So S1 covers (2,1) and (3,2). Good.\n\nS2 passes through (2,3) and (3,1) maybe? Slope = (1-3)/(3-2) = -2 => line y = -2(x-2) + 3 = -2x + 7. At x=3, y = -6 + 7 = 1, correct. At x=4, y = -8 + 7 = -1, not needed. So S2 covers (2,3) and (3,1). Good.\n\nNow we need to cover column 4 point (4,1). Neither S1 nor S2 passes through (4,1). S1: y = x - 1 => at x=4, y=3, not (4,1). S2: y = -2x + 7 => at x=4, y = -8 + 7 = -1, not (4,1). Horizontal line y=2 does not cover (4,1). So (4,1) remains uncovered. We could try to adjust the non-sunny line to be horizontal y=1, covering (4,1) and maybe (2,1) and (3,1). But then (2,1) and (3,1) would be covered by horizontal line, leaving them not needed for sunny lines. Let's try that.\n\nOption: Use vertical line x=1, horizontal line y=1 (non-sunny). Then column 1 is covered by vertical line. Horizontal line y=1 covers (2,1),(3,1),(4,1). So column 2's point (2,1) is covered, column 3's point (3,1) covered, column 4's point (4,1) covered. Then we need to cover remaining points: (2,2),(2,3),(3,2). That's three points. We have two sunny lines left. Each sunny line can cover at most one point per column. So we need to cover (2,2) and (3,2) maybe with one sunny line each, and (2,3) with the other sunny line? But we have only two sunny lines, so we can cover at most two points in column 2 (since each sunny line can cover at most one point in column 2). Column 2 has three points: (2,1) already covered by horizontal line, leaving (2,2) and (2,3). So we need to cover both (2,2) and (2,3) with the two sunny lines. That's okay: one sunny line covers (2,2), the other covers (2,3). Then we also need to cover (3,2). But we have no sunny line left (both used for column 2). However, we could have a sunny line that covers both (2,2) and (3,2) (since they are in different columns). That would free up a sunny line for (2,3). Let's try.\n\nLet S1 be line through (2,2) and (3,2). That's horizontal line y=2, which is not sunny. So not allowed. So we cannot have a sunny line covering both (2,2) and (3,2) because they have same y coordinate, which would be horizontal. So we need separate lines for (2,2) and (3,2). But we have only two sunny lines. So we could assign S1 to cover (2,2) and maybe some other point, S2 to cover (3,2) and maybe (2,3). But can a line cover (3,2) and (2,3)? Slope = (3-2)/(2-3) = 1/(-1) = -1, which is not sunny. So not allowed. So we cannot cover (3,2) and (2,3) with a sunny line. So we need a third sunny line to cover (3,2) or (2,3). So k=2 seems impossible for n=4.\n\nThus k=2 is impossible for n=4. So the solution's claim that k=2 is impossible for all n >= 3 seems plausible.\n\nNow, what about k=3? The solution gave a construction for all n >= 3. Let's verify for n=5 as well. For n=5, columns: sizes: column 5 size 1, column 4 size 2, column 3 size 3, column 2 size 4, column 1 size 5. The construction for k=3 uses three sunny lines covering points (n,1),(n-1,1),(n-2,1),(n-1,2),(n-2,2),(n-2,3). For n=5, that would be points: (5,1),(4,1),(3,1),(4,2),(3,2),(3,3). Then vertical lines for columns 1,2 (since n-3 = 2). So vertical lines x=1 and x=2. Let's check coverage:\n\n- Column 5: (5,1) covered by L1 (maybe). L1: y-1 = -1/2 (x-5). At x=5, y=1. At x=3, y-1 = -1/2 (3-5) = -1/2 * (-2) = 1 => y=2, so (3,2) is on L1? Actually (3,2) is covered by L3 maybe. Let's compute each line:\n\nL1: y-1 = -1/2 (x-5). So y = -0.5 x + 2.5 + 1 = -0.5 x + 3.5. At x=5, y=1. At x=4, y = -2 + 3.5 = 1.5, not integer. At x=3, y = -1.5 + 3.5 = 2, so (3,2) is on L1. At x=2, y = -1 + 3.5 = 2.5, not integer. So L1 covers (5,1) and (3,2). Good.\n\nL2: y-1 = -2 (x-(4)). So y = -2x + 8 + 1 = -2x + 9. At x=4, y=1. At x=3, y = -6 + 9 = 3, so (3,3) is on L2. At x=5, y = -10 + 9 = -1, not needed. So L2 covers (4,1) and (3,3). Good.\n\nL3: y-1 = (x-(3)). So y = x - 3 + 1 = x - 2. At x=3, y=1. At x=4, y=2, so (4,2) is on L3. At x=5, y=3, but (5,3) not in T_5 because 5+3=8 >6. So L3 covers (3,1) and (4,2). So the three sunny lines cover points: (5,1) (L1), (4,1) (L2), (3,1) (L3), (4,2) (L3), (3,2) (L1), (3,3) (L2). That's exactly the six points listed. So they are covered.\n\nNow vertical lines x=1 and x=2 cover all points in columns 1 and 2: column 1 points (1,1)-(1,5) covered by x=1; column 2 points (2,1)-(2,4) covered by x=2. So all points are covered. So k=3 works for n=5.\n\nThus the construction works for all n >= 3.\n\nNow, we need to verify that k=0,1,3 are indeed the only possible values. The solution's reasoning for impossibility of k=2 and k>=4 is somewhat informal but uses valid reasoning: each sunny line can intersect each column at most one point; thus to cover columns with many points we need vertical lines; there are constraints on number of vertical lines. However, we need to check if there is any possible configuration with k=4 for some n > 3 that the solution missed. Let's try to see if we can find a configuration for n=5 with k=4. That would be 5 lines total, 4 sunny, 1 non-sunny (vertical/horizontal/slope -1). Could we cover all points? Let's try to see if we can assign vertical line to column 1 (size 5). Then we have 4 sunny lines to cover columns 2-5. Column 2 has 4 points, column 3 has 3 points, column 4 has 2 points, column 5 has 1 point. With 4 sunny lines, each column can have at most 4 points covered by sunny lines (since each line can cover at most one point per column). So column 2's 4 points could be covered by the 4 sunny lines, each covering a distinct point. Column 3's 3 points could be covered by 3 of the sunny lines (maybe overlapping with column 2 coverage). Column 4's 2 points could be covered by 2 sunny lines. Column 5's 1 point covered by 1 sunny line. So in principle, the sunny lines could cover all points in columns 2-5, leaving column 1 covered by vertical line. So k=4 might be possible for n=5. Let's try to construct such a configuration.\n\nWe need 4 sunny lines, each passing through a distinct point in column 2 (to cover all 4 points). Let's denote column 2 points: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4). We need each sunny line to pass through one of these points. Additionally, each sunny line can also pass through points in other columns. We need to cover all points in columns 3,4,5 as well.\n\nLet's denote the 4 sunny lines as L1, L2, L3, L4. L1 passes through (2,1), L2 through (2,2), L3 through (2,3), L4 through (2,4). They must have distinct slopes (or else they'd be the same line). They can be any slopes not equal to 0, infinite, -1.\n\nNow we need to ensure that each point in column 3 (3,1),(3,2),(3,3) is covered by at least one of these lines. Similarly for column 4: (4,1),(4,2). Column 5: (5,1). Let's see if we can assign slopes to achieve this.\n\nWe can treat each line as defined by its slope m_i and passing through (2,i) where i = 1,2,3,4. So line L_i: y - i = m_i (x - 2). So y = m_i (x - 2) + i.\n\nNow we need to check for each point (c,b) in columns 3-5, whether there exists i such that b = m_i (c - 2) + i. That is, m_i = (b - i) / (c - 2). So for each point, we need to find i such that the slope m_i equals that ratio.\n\nThus we need to choose slopes m_i such that the set of slopes includes the needed ratios for each point.\n\nLet's list all required ratios:\n\nFor column 3 (c=3):\n- (3,1): ratio = (1 - i) / (3-2) = 1 - i. So for i=1: ratio = 0; i=2: ratio = -1; i=3: ratio = -2; i=4: ratio = -3.\n- (3,2): ratio = (2 - i) / 1 = 2 - i. So i=1: 1; i=2: 0; i=3: -1; i=4: -2.\n- (3,3): ratio = (3 - i) / 1 = 3 - i. So i=1: 2; i=2: 1; i=3: 0; i=4: -1.\n\nFor column 4 (c=4):\n- (4,1): ratio = (1 - i) / (4-2) = (1 - i) / 2. So i=1: 0; i=2: -0.5; i=3: -1; i=4: -1.5.\n- (4,2): ratio = (2 - i) / 2 = (2 - i)/2. So i=1: 0.5; i=2: 0; i=3: -0.5; i=4: -1.\n\nFor column 5 (c=5):\n- (5,1): ratio = (1 - i) / (5-2) = (1 - i) / 3. So i=1: 0; i=2: -1/3; i=3: -2/3; i=4: -1.\n\nNow we need to choose slopes m_i for i=1..4 such that each point is covered by at least one line. Since each line can cover multiple points across columns, we need to assign slopes that match many of these ratios.\n\nWe can try to pick slopes that are common across many points. For example, slope 0 (horizontal) is not allowed (sunny lines cannot be horizontal). So we cannot use slope 0. Slope -1 is also not allowed. So we cannot use slopes 0 or -1. So we must avoid those.\n\nNow, for i=1 (line through (2,1)), the ratio for (3,1) is 0 (horizontal), not allowed. So line L1 cannot cover (3,1) because that would be horizontal. For (3,2), ratio = 1, allowed (slope 1). For (3,3), ratio = 2, allowed. For (4,1), ratio = 0 (horizontal), not allowed. For (4,2), ratio = 0.5, allowed. For (5,1), ratio = 0 (horizontal), not allowed.\n\nThus L1 could be slope 1, covering (3,2). It could also be slope 2, covering (3,3). It could be slope 0.5, covering (4,2). It could be slope something else to cover other points.\n\nSimilarly for i=2 (line through (2,2)), ratios: (3,1): -1 (not allowed), (3,2): 0 (horizontal, not allowed), (3,3): 1 (allowed), (4,1): -0.5 (allowed), (4,2): 0 (horizontal, not allowed), (5,1): -1/3 (allowed). So L2 could be slope 1 to cover (3,3). Could be slope -0.5 to cover (4,1). Could be slope -1/3 to cover (5,1). But slope -0.5 is allowed (not -1). So L2 could be slope -0.5.\n\nFor i=3 (line through (2,3)), ratios: (3,1): -2 (allowed), (3,2): -1 (not allowed), (3,3): 0 (horizontal, not allowed), (4,1): -1 (not allowed), (4,2): -0.5 (allowed), (5,1): -2/3 (allowed). So L3 could be slope -2 to cover (3,1). Could be slope -0.5 to cover (4,2). Could be slope -2/3 to cover (5,1). So L3 could be slope -2.\n\nFor i=4 (line through (2,4)), ratios: (3,1): -3 (allowed), (3,2): -2 (allowed), (3,3): -1 (not allowed), (4,1): -1.5 (allowed), (4,2): -1 (not allowed), (5,1): -1 (not allowed). So L4 could be slope -3 to cover (3,1). Could be slope -2 to cover (3,2). Could be slope -1.5 to cover (4,1). So L4 could be slope -2 (but slope -2 already used by L3 maybe). But lines can have same slope but different intercept? Actually if they have same slope but different intercept, they are distinct lines. So it's okay to have same slope.\n\nNow we need to cover all points. Let's try to assign slopes:\n\n- L1: slope 1 (covers (3,2)). Also passes through (2,1). At x=4, y = 1*(4-2)+1 = 3, so (4,3) not in T_5. At x=5, y = 1*(5-2)+1 = 4, (5,4) not in T_5. So L1 covers (2,1) and (3,2). Good.\n\n- L2: slope -0.5 (covers (4,1)). Let's compute: line through (2,2) with slope -0.5: y - 2 = -0.5 (x - 2) => y = -0.5 x + 1 + 2 = -0.5 x + 3. At x=4, y = -2 + 3 = 1 => (4,1) covered. At x=3, y = -1.5 + 3 = 1.5, not integer. At x=5, y = -2.5 + 3 = 0.5, not integer. So L2 covers (2,2) and (4,1). Good.\n\n- L3: slope -2 (covers (3,1)). Through (2,3): y - 3 = -2 (x - 2) => y = -2x + 4 + 3 = -2x + 7. At x=3, y = -6 + 7 = 1 => (3,1) covered. At x=4, y = -8 + 7 = -1, not needed. At x=5, y = -10 + 7 = -3. So L3 covers (2,3) and (3,1). Good.\n\n- L4: slope -2 (through (2,4)): y - 4 = -2 (x - 2) => y = -2x + 4 + 4 = -2x + 8. At x=3, y = -6 + 8 = 2 => (3,2) covered. At x=4, y = -8 + 8 = 0, not needed. At x=5, y = -10 + 8 = -2. So L4 covers (2,4) and (3,2). Good.\n\nNow let's see which points are covered:\n\n- Column 2: all points (2,1) L1, (2,2) L2, (2,3) L3, (2,4) L4. Good.\n\n- Column 3: (3,1) L3, (3,2) L4, (3,3) not covered yet. (3,3) is not covered. Could any line cover (3,3)? Let's check each line:\n\nL1: y = x - 1 (since slope 1, passes through (2,1)). At x=3, y=2, not 3. L2: y = -0.5 x + 3. At x=3, y = -1.5 + 3 = 1.5, not integer. L3: y = -2x + 7. At x=3, y=1, not 3. L4: y = -2x + 8. At x=3, y=2, not 3. So (3,3) is uncovered.\n\n- Column 4: (4,1) L2, (4,2) not covered. Check lines: L1 at x=4 gives y=3, not 2. L2 at x=4 gives y=1, not 2. L3 at x=4 gives y=-1, not 2. L4 at x=4 gives y=0, not 2. So (4,2) uncovered.\n\n- Column 5: (5,1) not covered. L1 at x=5 gives y=4, not 1. L2 at x=5 gives y=0.5, not 1. L3 at x=5 gives y=-3, not 1. L4 at x=5 gives y=-2, not 1.\n\nThus this assignment fails to cover many points.\n\nWe could try different slopes. Let's try to see if we can cover all points with 4 sunny lines. This is a combinatorial design problem. Let's attempt to systematically search for a solution for n=5, k=4. Since we are not writing code, we can try to reason about constraints.\n\nWe have 4 sunny lines, each can intersect each column at most once. So each column can have at most 4 points covered by sunny lines. Column 2 has 4 points, so all must be covered by sunny lines (since we have only one non-sunny line, which we could use for column 1). So each sunny line must pass through a distinct point in column 2. So we have exactly one sunny line per point in column 2. So the mapping is forced: each sunny line passes through a unique point in column 2.\n\nNow, each sunny line can also intersect columns 3,4,5 at at most one point each. So we need to assign each point in columns 3-5 to some sunny line such that each line's intersection with each column is at most one point. This is essentially a matching problem: we have 4 lines (L1..L4) and points in column 3 (3 points), column 4 (2 points), column 5 (1 point). We need to assign each point to a line such that no line gets more than one point per column. Since there are 4 lines, we can assign each point to a distinct line, but we must also respect that each line already has a point in column 2 (its anchor). So each line can have at most one point in each column besides column 2.\n\nThus we need to assign the 3+2+1 = 6 points across columns 3-5 to the 4 lines, with each line possibly covering up to 3 points (one per column). Since there are 4 lines, total capacity is 4*3 = 12, which is enough. So capacity is not a problem. The difficulty is that the geometry must allow each line to pass through its assigned points.\n\nThus we need to find a set of slopes m_i such that for each line i (through (2,i)), the points assigned to it satisfy the slope condition.\n\nWe can think of each line i as having slope m_i. For each column c > 2, the point (c,b) assigned to line i must satisfy b = m_i (c - 2) + i. So m_i = (b - i) / (c - 2). So for each line i, the set of possible points it can cover are those where the ratio (b - i) / (c - 2) is equal to the chosen slope m_i.\n\nThus each line i can cover at most one point per column, but we can choose which point per column by selecting m_i appropriately. So we need to choose m_i such that for each column c, the set of points (c,b) assigned to line i yields the same m_i across all assigned columns for that line.\n\nThus each line i must have a slope m_i that matches the ratio for each assigned point. So we need to find a slope m_i that simultaneously matches the ratio for possibly multiple points across different columns. For each line i, we can assign at most one point per column, but we can assign multiple columns to the same line if the slope matches the required ratio for each column's assigned point.\n\nThus we need to find a set of slopes m_i (i=1..4) such that for each column c=3,4,5, each point (c,b) is assigned to some line i where m_i = (b - i) / (c - 2). This is like a system of equations.\n\nWe can try to find a solution manually. Let's denote the points we need to cover:\n\nColumn 3: (3,1),(3,2),(3,3)\nColumn 4: (4,1),(4,2)\nColumn 5: (5,1)\n\nWe need to assign each of these points to a line i (1..4). Let's denote assignment function f(c,b) = i.\n\nWe need to ensure that for each line i, the slopes computed from each assigned point are equal. That is, for any two points (c1,b1) and (c2,b2) assigned to same line i, we must have (b1 - i) / (c1 - 2) = (b2 - i) / (c2 - 2). This is a linear condition.\n\nThus each line i can cover at most one point per column, but can cover multiple columns if the points lie on a line with slope m_i.\n\nThus we need to partition the 6 points into at most 4 groups (lines), each group containing at most one point per column, and each group must be collinear with the anchor point (2,i). So each group must be a set of points that lie on a line through (2,i). So we need to find lines through each (2,i) that pass through some of the points.\n\nLet's try to find lines through (2,1) that pass through some points:\n\n- Through (2,1) and (3,1): slope = (1-1)/(3-2) = 0 (horizontal) not allowed.\n- Through (2,1) and (3,2): slope = (2-1)/(3-2) = 1, allowed. This line also passes through (4,3) (since slope 1, intercept -1). (4,3) not in T_5 (since 4+3=7 >6). So line L1: (2,1)-(3,2) covers (2,1) and (3,2). It also passes through (5,4) not needed. So L1 can cover (3,2). It could also cover (4,3) but not needed.\n\n- Through (2,1) and (3,3): slope = (3-1)/(3-2) = 2, allowed. This line passes through (4,5) not in T_5. So L1 could also be slope 2 covering (3,3). But we cannot have both (3,2) and (3,3) on same line because they have different slopes. So we need to choose which point to assign to line through (2,1). So we can assign either (3,2) or (3,3) (or (4,1) maybe). Let's compute slopes for other columns:\n\n- Through (2,1) and (4,1): slope = (1-1)/(4-2) = 0 (horizontal) not allowed.\n- Through (2,1) and (4,2): slope = (2-1)/(4-2) = 1/2 = 0.5, allowed. So line through (2,1) and (4,2) has slope 0.5. It also passes through (6,3) not needed. So L1 could cover (4,2) as well.\n\n- Through (2,1) and (5,1): slope = (1-1)/(5-2) = 0 (horizontal) not allowed.\n\nThus line through (2,1) can cover either (3,2) (slope 1), (3,3) (slope 2), (4,2) (slope 0.5). It cannot cover (3,1), (4,1), (5,1) because those would be horizontal.\n\nNow line through (2,2) can cover:\n\n- (3,1): slope = (1-2)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed.\n- (3,2): slope = (2-2)/(3-2) = 0, not allowed.\n- (3,3): slope = (3-2)/(3-2) = 1, allowed. So line (2,2)-(3,3) slope 1.\n\n- (4,1): slope = (1-2)/(4-2) = -1/2 = -0.5, allowed. So line (2,2)-(4,1) slope -0.5.\n\n- (4,2): slope = (2-2)/(4-2) = 0, not allowed.\n\n- (5,1): slope = (1-2)/(5-2) = -1/3 ≈ -0.333..., allowed.\n\nThus line through (2,2) can cover (3,3) (slope 1), (4,1) (slope -0.5), (5,1) (slope -1/3). It cannot cover (3,1),(3,2),(4,2).\n\nLine through (2,3) can cover:\n\n- (3,1): slope = (1-3)/(3-2) = -2, allowed.\n- (3,2): slope = (2-3)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed.\n- (3,3): slope = (3-3)/(3-2) = 0, not allowed.\n- (4,1): slope = (1-3)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed.\n- (4,2): slope = (2-3)/(4-2) = -1/2 = -0.5, allowed.\n- (5,1): slope = (1-3)/(5-2) = -2/3 ≈ -0.666..., allowed.\n\nThus line through (2,3) can cover (3,1) (slope -2), (4,2) (slope -0.5), (5,1) (slope -2/3). It cannot cover (3,2),(3,3),(4,1).\n\nLine through (2,4) can cover:\n\n- (3,1): slope = (1-4)/(3-2) = -3, allowed.\n- (3,2): slope = (2-4)/(3-2) = -2, allowed.\n- (3,3): slope = (3-4)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed.\n- (4,1): slope = (1-4)/(4-2) = -3/2 = -1.5, allowed.\n- (4,2): slope = (2-4)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed.\n- (5,1): slope = (1-4)/(5-2) = -3/3 = -1, not allowed.\n\nThus line through (2,4) can cover (3,1) (slope -3), (3,2) (slope -2), (4,1) (slope -1.5). It cannot cover (3,3),(4,2),(5,1).\n\nNow we need to assign each point in columns 3-5 to one of these lines, respecting that each line can cover at most one point per column.\n\nLet's list possible assignments:\n\n- (3,1): can be covered by lines through (2,3) (slope -2) or (2,4) (slope -3). Not by (2,1) or (2,2) because those would be horizontal or slope -1.\n\n- (3,2): can be covered by lines through (2,1) (slope 1), (2,4) (slope -2). Not by (2,2) (horizontal), (2,3) (slope -1 not allowed). So options: L1 (i=1) slope 1, L4 (i=4) slope -2.\n\n- (3,3): can be covered by lines through (2,1) (slope 2), (2,2) (slope 1). Not by (2,3) (horizontal), (2,4) (slope -1 not allowed). So options: L1 (i=1) slope 2, L2 (i=2) slope 1.\n\n- (4,1): can be covered by lines through (2,2) (slope -0.5), (2,4) (slope -1.5). Not by (2,1) (horizontal), (2,3) (slope -1 not allowed). So options: L2 (i=2) slope -0.5, L4 (i=4) slope -1.5.\n\n- (4,2): can be covered by lines through (2,1) (slope 0.5), (2,3) (slope -0.5). Not by (2,2) (horizontal), (2,4) (slope -1 not allowed). So options: L1 (i=1) slope 0.5, L3 (i=3) slope -0.5.\n\n- (5,1): can be covered by lines through (2,2) (slope -1/3), (2,3) (slope -2/3). Not by (2,1) (horizontal), (2,4) (slope -1 not allowed). So options: L2 (i=2) slope -1/3, L3 (i=3) slope -2/3.\n\nNow we need to assign each point to a line such that each line gets at most one point per column. Let's try to find a consistent assignment.\n\nWe have 4 lines: L1 (i=1), L2 (i=2), L3 (i=3), L4 (i=4). Let's denote which points we assign to each.\n\nWe need to assign each of the 6 points to some line.\n\nLet's try to assign:\n\n- (3,1) -> L3 (i=3) slope -2 (since L3 can cover (3,1) with slope -2). That uses column 3 for L3.\n\n- (3,2) -> L1 (i=1) slope 1 (L1 can cover (3,2)). That uses column 3 for L1.\n\n- (3,3) -> L2 (i=2) slope 1 (L2 can cover (