le slope. Indeed slope from (2,2) to (5,1) is (1-2)/(5-2) = -1/3, not 1. So L2 cannot cover both (3,3) and (5,1). So we need to adjust assignments.\n\nThus we need to assign points to lines such that each line's assigned points are collinear with its anchor.\n\nLet's try a different assignment.\n\nWe need to assign each line i a slope m_i. For each line i, we can assign at most one point per column, but the slope must be consistent across all assigned points.\n\nThus we need to choose for each line i a slope m_i that matches the ratio for each assigned point.\n\nWe can treat each line i as a candidate slope m_i. For each line i, we can choose a slope from the set of possible slopes that correspond to points we want to cover. Then we need to ensure that each point is covered by some line i with matching slope.\n\nThus we need to find a set of slopes m_1,...,m_4 such that for each point (c,b) there exists i with m_i = (b - i) / (c - 2). This is like a hitting set problem.\n\nLet's list all possible slopes for each line i:\n\ni=1 (anchor (2,1)):\n- (3,2): slope 1\n- (3,3): slope 2\n- (4,2): slope 0.5\n- (4,3): slope 1 (but (4,3) not in T_5)\n- (5,2): slope (2-1)/(5-2) = 1/3 ≈ 0.333... (but (5,2) not in T_5 because 5+2=7 >6)\n- (5,3): slope (3-1)/3 = 2/3 ≈ 0.666... (not in T_5)\n- (5,4): slope (4-1)/3 = 1, but (5,4) not in T_5.\n\nThus possible slopes for i=1 that cover points in T_5 are: 1 (covers (3,2)), 2 (covers (3,3)), 0.5 (covers (4,2)). Also slope 0 (horizontal) would cover (3,1),(4,1),(5,1) but not allowed. So i=1 can cover (3,2) with slope 1, (3,3) with slope 2, (4,2) with slope 0.5.\n\ni=2 (anchor (2,2)):\n- (3,3): slope 1\n- (4,1): slope -0.5\n- (5,1): slope -1/3\n- (3,2): slope 0 (horizontal) not allowed\n- (3,1): slope -1 (not allowed)\n- (4,2): slope 0 (horizontal) not allowed\n- (4,3): slope 0.5 (but (4,3) not in T_5)\n- (5,2): slope 0 (horizontal) not allowed\n- (5,3): slope 0.333... (but (5,3) not in T_5)\nThus possible slopes for i=2: 1 (covers (3,3)),