here are n+1-i points. Let V be the set of vertical lines among the non-sunny lines. Let H be horizontal lines among non-sunny lines. Let D be diagonal lines among non-sunny lines.\n\nIf we have v vertical lines, they cover v columns completely. So those columns are fully covered. The remaining columns (n - v) have points that must be covered by other lines: horizontal, diagonal, sunny.\n\nHorizontal lines y = d cover points across columns: for each column i, they cover point (i,d) if i+d ≤ n+1 (i.e., i ≤ n+1-d). So a horizontal line covers at most n+1-d points, one per column up to column n+1-d.\n\nDiagonal lines x+y = s cover points across columns: for each column i, they cover point (i, s-i) if s-i ≥ 1 and i + (s-i) = s ≤ n+1 => i ≤ s-1 and s-i ≥ 1 => i ≤ s-1 and i ≥ s-(n+1)?? Actually s-i ≥ 1 => i ≤ s-1. So diagonal line covers points for columns i from max(1, s-(n+1)) to min(n, s-1). So it covers at most min(s-1, n) - max(1, s-(n+1)) + 1 points, which is roughly s-1 for s ≤ n+1, else n+1-s for s > n+1? Actually s ranges from 2 to n+1 (since points have a+b ≤ n+1). So diagonal lines x+y = s for s in [2, n+1] cover s-1 points (one per column i from 1 to s-1). So diagonal lines also cover at most one point per column, but only for columns up to s-1.\n\nThus both horizontal and diagonal lines also cover at most one point per column (like sunny lines). So the only line type that can cover multiple points in a column is vertical lines. So to cover all points, we need enough vertical lines to cover the columns with many points. But we have only n-k non-sunny lines total, which may include vertical lines. So we need to allocate vertical lines to cover columns with many points, and use other lines (horizontal, diagonal, sunny) to cover the rest.\n\nThus the problem reduces to: given n columns with decreasing numbers of points, we have n lines total, of which k are sunny (cover at most one point per column). The remaining n-k lines are non-sunny, which can be vertical (cover all points in a column) or horizontal/diagonal (cover at most one point per column). So the only way to cover more than one point per column is to use vertical lines. So we need enough vertical lines to cover columns that have more than (k+1?) points? Let's think.\n\nIf we have k sunny lines, each column can have at most k points covered by sunny lines. Additionally, each horizontal or diagonal line can cover at most one point per column. So overall, for each column, the total number of points covered by non-vertical lines (including sunny, horizontal, diagonal) is at most (k + (n-k - v)), where v is number of vertical lines among non-sunny lines. Actually we have n-k non-sunny lines, of which v are vertical, and the rest (n-k - v) are horizontal or diagonal. Each of those (n-k - v) lines can cover at most one point per column. So total points per column that can be covered by non-vertical lines is at most k + (n-k - v) = n - v. So each column can have at most n - v points covered by non-vertical lines (including sunny). The remaining points in that column must be covered by vertical lines (if any). But each vertical line covers all points in its column. So if we have v vertical lines, we can cover v columns completely. For those columns, we don't need any other lines. For the remaining n - v columns, we need to cover all points using only non-vertical lines (sunny, horizontal, diagonal). But each column has at most n - v points that can be covered by non-vertical lines (since each non-vertical line can contribute at most one point per column). However, the number of points in column i is n+1-i. So we need n+1-i ≤ n - v for each column i that is not covered by a vertical line. That is, for each uncovered column i, we need n+1-i ≤ n - v => i ≥ v+1. So columns with index i ≥ v+1 must satisfy i ≥ v+1, which is always true. But we need to ensure that the number of points in column i does not exceed n - v. Let's compute n+1-i ≤ n - v => i ≥ v+1. So for any column i 