 k sunny lines (i.e., lines not parallel to x-axis, y-axis, or line x+y=0). So non-sunny lines are those parallel to x-axis (horizontal), y-axis (vertical), or line x+y=0 (i.e., slope -1). So we have three families of non-sunny lines: horizontal, vertical, and slope -1 lines.\n\nWe need to find all possible k for which such a configuration exists.\n\nWe need to find possible numbers of sunny lines among n lines covering all points.\n\nWe need to see if answer {0,1,3} is correct.\n\nWe need to check if k=2 is impossible, and k≥4 is impossible. The solution says k can only be 0,1,3. It doesn't discuss k≥4, but says \"k can only be 0,1, or 3\". So they claim k≥4 impossible.\n\nWe need to verify if indeed k≥4 is impossible. Also need to verify if k=0,1,3 are possible.\n\nWe need to check if there exist constructions for k=0,1,3 for any n≥3.\n\nThe solution gives constructions: k=0: vertical lines x=1,...,x=n. That covers all points? Let's check: vertical line x=1 covers points (1,b) for b from 1 to n? Actually b must satisfy a+b ≤ n+1, so for a=1, b can be up to n. So vertical line x=1 covers points (1,b) for b=1,...,n. Similarly x=2 covers (2,b) for b=1,...,n-1, etc. So vertical lines x=1,...,x=n cover all points indeed. So k=0 works.\n\nk=1: vertical lines x=1,...,x=n-1 plus a sunny line through (n,1). Does that cover all points? Let's see: vertical lines x=1,...,x=n-1 cover all points with a ≤ n-1. The only points left are those with a=n. For a=n, b must satisfy n+b ≤ n+1 => b ≤ 1. So only point (n,1) is left. So we need a line covering (n,1). The sunny line through (n,1) can be any line not parallel to x-axis, y-axis, or x+y=0. So we can choose a line that passes through (n,1) and maybe also other points? But we only need to cover (n,1). So a sunny line through (n,1) works. So k=1 works.\n\nk=3: three sunny lines cover the 6 rightmost points, verticals cover the rest. Let's examine. The \"rightmost points\" presumably are points with a≥n-2? Let's list points for a+b ≤ n+1. The rightmost columns are x=n-2, n-1, n. For x=n-2, b can be up to 3 (since n-2 + b ≤ n+1 => b ≤ 3). So points: (n-2,1), (n-2,2), (n-2,3). For x=n-1, b ≤ 2: (n-1,1), (n-1,2). For x=n, b ≤ 1: (n,1). So total of 3+2+1 = 6 points. The solution says three sunny lines cover these 6 points. Is that possible? Let's see if we can cover these 6 points with three sunny lines. Since each line can cover at most 2 of these points? Actually a line can pass through multiple points. Let's see if we can find three sunny lines that cover all six points. For example, we could use lines of slope 1 (i.e., x-y = constant) or slope -1? Wait slope -1 lines are not sunny (they are non-sunny). So sunny lines cannot be slope -1, cannot be vertical, cannot be horizontal. So they can have any other slope, e.g., slope 1, slope 2, etc. Let's see if we can cover these points with three sunny lines. For instance, line through (n,1) and (n-1,2) has slope (2-1)/(n-1 - n) = 1/(-1) = -1, which is slope -1, not allowed (non-sunny). So we cannot use that line. Similarly line through (n,1) and (n-2,3) has slope (3-1)/(n-2 - n) = 2/(-2) = -1, also not allowed. So we cannot connect (n,1) with (n-1,2) or (n-2,3) using a sunny line because those are slope -1 lines. So we need to find other lines.\n\nLet's try to cover (n,1) with a sunny line that also covers some other point(s). Since slope -1 is forbidden, we cannot use line connecting (n,1) to any point (a,b) where a+b = n+1? Actually slope -1 lines are those where x+y = constant. So any line connecting two points that satisfy a+b = constant will have slope -1. Indeed, points (n,1) and (n-1,2) both satisfy a+b = n+1, so they lie on line x+y = n+1, which is slope -1, not allowed. Similarly (n-2,3) also satisfies a+b = n+1, so all points in the rightmost column are on the line x+y = n+1. So that line is non-sunny. So we cannot use that line. So we need to cover these points with sunny lines that are not slope -1. So we cannot have a line that contains two points from the same \"anti-diagonal\" (i.e., same sum a+b). So each sunny line can contain at most one point from each anti-diagonal. But we have only one anti-diagonal for these points: all have a+b = n+1. So we cannot have a sunny line that contains more than one of these points. So we need at least 6 sunny lines to cover them if we cannot use non-sunny lines. But we have only 3 sunny lines allowed. However, we can also use non-sunny lines (vertical, horizontal, slope -1) among the n lines. The solution says \"three sunny lines cover the 6 rightmost points, verticals cover the rest.\" That suggests they intend to use three sunny lines to cover the six points, presumably each sunny line covering two points. But as we argued, any two points among those six have sum a+b = n+1, so they lie on the line x+y = n+1, which is slope -1, which is non-sunny. So we cannot have a sunny line covering two of those points. So the claim that three sunny lines can cover the six rightmost points seems false. However, maybe they intend to use also non-sunny lines among the three sunny lines? Wait they said \"three sunny lines cover the 6 rightmost points, verticals cover the rest.\" So they propose using three sunny lines to cover the six points. But as we argued, each sunny line can cover at most one of those points, because any line connecting two of them would be slope -1. So we would need six sunny lines to cover them, not three. So the construction for k=3 seems flawed.\n\nBut perhaps they intend to use also non-sunny lines among the verticals to cover some of those points. Let's examine: vertical lines x=n-2, x=n-1, x=n. But vertical lines are non-sunny. So we could use vertical lines to cover some of those points. But the construction says \"three sunny lines cover the 6 rightmost points, verticals cover the rest.\" That suggests they are using vertical lines for the rest of the points (i.e., columns x=1,...,n-3). So they are using vertical lines for columns left of n-2. So they are not using vertical lines for the rightmost columns. So they need to cover the six points in columns n-2, n-1, n using three sunny lines. That seems impossible.\n\nBut maybe they can use a sunny line that is not vertical/horizontal/slope -1, but passes through two points that are not on the same anti-diagonal? But all six points are on the same anti-diagonal (sum = n+1). So any line connecting any two of them will have slope -1. So indeed any line through any two of them is non-sunny. So a sunny line can contain at most one of those points. So we need at least six sunny lines to cover them. So k=3 is impossible. But the solution claims k=3 works.\n\nThus the answer {0,1,3} is likely incorrect. The correct answer might be {0,1} only? Or maybe {0,1,2}? Let's think.\n\nWe need to find all possible k. Let's try to reason from scratch.\n\nWe have n lines covering all points. There are n(n+1)/2 points. Each line can cover many points. We need to find constraints on number of sunny lines.\n\nWe have three families of non-sunny lines: vertical (parallel to y-axis), horizontal (parallel to x-axis), and slope -1 lines (parallel to x+y=0). Let's denote them as V, H, D (for diagonal). Sunny lines are any other slopes.\n\nWe need to cover all points (a,b) with a+b ≤ n+1.\n\nObservation: Points with same a coordinate (i.e., same column) are (a,b) for b=1,...,n+1-a. So column a has n+1-a points. Similarly, rows (b coordinate) have n+1-b points.\n\nIf we use a vertical line x=a, it covers all points in column a. So vertical lines are efficient for covering columns.\n\nIf we use a horizontal line y=b, it covers all points in row b.\n\nIf we use a diagonal line x+y = c (c constant), it covers all points on anti-diagonal c. For c from 2 to n+1 (since a,b≥1). So diagonal lines are also efficient.\n\nSunny lines have arbitrary slopes, but they cannot be vertical/horizontal/diagonal. They can cover at most one point per column (since they are not vertical) and at most one point per row (since not horizontal). They also cannot be diagonal, so they cannot contain two points with same sum a+b. So each sunny line can contain at most one point from each column, each row, each anti-diagonal. So each sunny line can cover at most one point from each column, row, anti-diagonal. But there are many points.\n\nThus sunny lines are less efficient than non-sunny lines. So we expect that the number of sunny lines is limited.\n\nWe need to find the maximum possible number of sunny lines (k) such that we can still cover all points with n lines total.\n\nWe also need to find possible values of k (maybe 0,1,2?). Let's try to find a construction for k=2. The solution says k=2 is impossible. But we need to verify.\n\nFirst, we need to see if we can cover all points with n lines, exactly 2 of which are sunny. Let's try to construct for general n.\n\nWe can use vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-2 (that's n-2 lines). Then we have left 2 lines to cover columns n-1 and n. Column n-1 has points (n-1,1) and (n-1,2). Column n has point (n,1). So we need to cover these three points with 2 lines, exactly 2 of which are sunny. So we need both remaining lines to be sunny. But we need to cover three points with two sunny lines. Since each sunny line can cover at most one point from each column, we could try to have one sunny line cover (n-1,1) and (n,1) maybe? But those two points have coordinates (n-1,1) and (n,1). They share same y coordinate (b=1). A line through them is horizontal (y=1), which is non-sunny. So cannot be sunny. So we cannot cover both (n-1,1) and (n,1) with a sunny line. Could we cover (n-1,2) and (n-1,1) with a sunny line? That would be vertical line x=n-1, non-sunny. So not allowed. Could we cover (n-1,2) and (n,1) with a sunny line? Let's compute slope: (1-2)/(n - (n-1)) = (-1)/1 = -1, which is diagonal, non-sunny. So not allowed. So any line connecting any two of these three points is either vertical, horizontal, or diagonal, all non-sunny. So any sunny line can cover at most one of these three points. So we need at least three sunny lines to cover them. So k=2 is impossible. So indeed k=2 is impossible.\n\nNow what about k=3? Could we have three sunny lines covering the six rightmost points? Let's examine the six points: (n-2,1), (n-2,2), (n-2,3), (n-1,1), (n-1,2), (n,1). As we argued, any line connecting any two of these points will be either vertical (if same x), horizontal (if same y), or diagonal (if sum constant). Let's check all pairs:\n\n- Points with same x: (n-2,1)-(n-2,2) vertical; (n-2,2)-(n-2,3) vertical; (n-1,1)-(n-1,2) vertical. So vertical lines are non-sunny.\n\n- Points with same y: (n-2,1)-(n-1,1) horizontal; (n-2,1)-(n,1) horizontal; (n-1,1)-(n,1) horizontal. So horizontal lines are non-sunny.\n\n- Points with same sum a+b = n+1: (n-2,3) sum = n+1; (n-1,2) sum = n+1; (n,1) sum = n+1. So line connecting any two of these three points is diagonal (slope -1), non-sunny.\n\n- Points (n-2,2) sum = n? Actually (n-2,2) sum = n, not n+1. So (n-2,2) is not on the diagonal line x+y=n+1. Let's check pairs involving (n-2,2). Pair (n-2,2)-(n-1,1): slope = (1-2)/(n-1 - (n-2)) = (-1)/1 = -1, diagonal, non-sunny. Pair (n-2,2)-(n,1): slope = (1-2)/(n - (n-2)) = (-1)/2 = -0.5, which is not -1, not vertical, not horizontal. So this line is not parallel to x-axis, y-axis, or x+y=0. So it's a sunny line! Indeed, slope -0.5 is not -1, so it's allowed. So a sunny line can pass through (n-2,2) and (n,1). That covers two points. Similarly, (n-2,2)-(n-2,1) is vertical, not allowed. (n-2,2)-(n-2,3) vertical, not allowed. (n-2,2)-(n-1,2) horizontal, not allowed. So the only possible sunny line through (n-2,2) that also covers another point among the six is (n-2,2)-(n,1). That line has slope -1/2. So we can cover (n-2,2) and (n,1) with a sunny line.\n\nNow consider (n-2,1). It can pair with (n-1,2): slope = (2-1)/(n-1 - (n-2)) = 1/1 = 1, which is slope 1, not vertical/horizontal/diagonal. So line through (n-2,1) and (n-1,2) is sunny. Indeed slope 1 is allowed (since not -1). So we can cover (n-2,1) and (n-1,2) with a sunny line.\n\nNow consider (n-2,3). It can pair with (n-1,1): slope = (1-3)/(n-1 - (n-2)) = -2/1 = -2, which is not -1, so it's sunny. So line through (n-2,3) and (n-1,1) is sunny.\n\nNow we have covered all six points with three sunny lines: (n-2,1)-(n-1,2), (n-2,2)-(n,1), (n-2,3)-(n-1,1). Let's verify each line is not parallel to x-axis, y-axis, or x+y=0. Slopes: 1, -0.5, -2. None are 0, undefined, or -1. So they are sunny. So indeed three sunny lines can cover the six points. So the solution's claim that three sunny lines can cover the six rightmost points is plausible. However, the solution didn't specify which lines; they just said \"three sunny lines cover the 6 rightmost points\". So the claim is plausible, but we need to verify that the three sunny lines indeed exist for any n≥3. The lines we gave involve points (n-2,1)-(n-1,2), (n-2,2)-(n,1), (n-2,3)-(n-1,1). These are valid for n≥3? Let's check n≥3: For n=3, the points are: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3?) Wait n=3 => points with a+b ≤ 4. So points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3?), (2,1),(2,2),(3,1). Actually (1,3) sum=4, allowed. So columns: x=1 has points (1,1),(1,2),(1,3). x=2 has (2,1),(2,2). x=3 has (3,1). So the rightmost columns are x=1? Actually n-2 = 1, n-1 = 2, n = 3. So the six points are exactly all points. So we need to cover all six points with three sunny lines. Let's see if the lines we gave work for n=3: (n-2,1) = (1,1) and (n-1,2) = (2,2) => line through (1,1) and (2,2) slope = 1, sunny. (n-2,2) = (1,2) and (n,1) = (3,1) => line slope = (1-2)/(3-1) = -1/2, sunny. (n-2,3) = (1,3) and (n-1,1) = (2,1) => slope = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2, sunny. So indeed three sunny lines cover all six points for n=3. So k=3 works for n=3.\n\nNow for n=4: points: a+b ≤5. Points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4); (2,1),(2,2),(2,3); (3,1),(3,2); (4,1). Rightmost columns: x=2 (n-2=2) has points (2,1),(2,2),(2,3). x=3 (n-1=3) has (3,1),(3,2). x=4 (n=4) has (4,1). So six points: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(3,1),(3,2),(4,1). The lines we gave: (n-2,1)-(n-1,2) => (2,1)-(3,2) slope = (2-1)/(3-2) = 1, sunny. (n-2,2)-(n,1) => (2,2)-(4,1) slope = (1-2)/(4-2) = -1/2, sunny. (n-2,3)-(n-1,1) => (2,3)-(3,1) slope = (1-3)/(3-2) = -2, sunny. So indeed three sunny lines cover all six points. So k=3 works for n=4.\n\nThus the construction for k=3 is plausible: use vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-3 (that's n-3 vertical lines) plus three sunny lines covering the six points in columns n-2,n-1,n. That yields total lines = (n-3) + 3 = n lines. So k=3 works for any n≥3.\n\nThus the solution's claim that k=3 works is correct, albeit not fully detailed. So the answer {0,1,3} might be correct.\n\nNow we need to verify if any other k values are possible. Could k be >3? Let's examine.\n\nWe need to see if we can have k=4 or more. The solution says \"k can only be 0,1, or 3\". They didn't discuss k≥4. We need to verify if k≥4 is impossible.\n\nWe need to see if we can have k=4. That would mean we have 4 sunny lines and n-4 non-sunny lines. We need to cover all points. The non-sunny lines can be vertical, horizontal, or diagonal. The most efficient use of non-sunny lines is to use vertical lines for columns, because each vertical line covers many points. Using horizontal lines might also be efficient, but we need to consider the constraints.\n\nWe have n lines total. If we have 4 sunny lines, we have n-4 non-sunny lines. The non-sunny lines can be vertical lines covering columns. The maximum number of columns we can cover with n-4 vertical lines is n-4 columns. There are n columns total (x=1,...,x=n). So we would need to cover the remaining 4 columns with the 4 sunny lines. However, each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, but can cover points across multiple columns. So we need to see if we can cover the points in the remaining columns with 4 sunny lines.\n\nAlternatively, we could use some horizontal or diagonal lines to cover some columns partially. But we need to see if any arrangement can achieve k=4.\n\nWe need to find a general bound on k. Let's try to derive a bound.\n\nWe have n lines covering all points. Let's denote the set of points as P = {(a,b) ∈ ℕ^2: a≥1, b≥1, a+b ≤ n+1}. There are N = n(n+1)/2 points.\n\nWe have n lines. Each line can be of three types: vertical (V), horizontal (H), diagonal (D), or sunny (S). Let's denote counts: v, h, d, s such that v+h+d+s = n, and s = k.\n\nWe need to cover all points. Let's consider the columns. For each column a (1 ≤ a ≤ n), there are n+1-a points. If we have a vertical line at x=a, it covers all points in that column. If we don't have a vertical line at x=a, then we need to cover those points using other lines (horizontal, diagonal, sunny). Let's consider the column with the most points: column a=1 has n points. Column a=2 has n-1 points, etc. So the columns have decreasing size.\n\nIf we have v vertical lines, we can cover v columns completely. The remaining n-v columns must be covered by other lines.\n\nNow, each horizontal line y=b covers all points in row b, which includes points from columns a=1,...,n+1-b. So a horizontal line covers points across many columns, but only one point per column (since each column has at most one point with that given y). So a horizontal line can cover at most one point per column. Similarly, a diagonal line x+y=c covers points across many columns, but again at most one point per column (since for each column a, there is at most one b such that a+b=c). So each non-vertical line (horizontal or diagonal) can cover at most one point per column.\n\nSimilarly, each sunny line can also cover at most one point per column (since it's not vertical). So any non-vertical line can cover at most one point per column.\n\nThus, for each column not covered by a vertical line, we need to cover its points using at most one point per line per column. So if we have t non-vertical lines (including horizontal, diagonal, sunny), each column can be covered at most t points (one per line). But each column has many points. So we need enough lines to cover all points in each column.\n\nSpecifically, for column a, we need to cover n+1-a points. If we have a vertical line at that column, it's done. If not, we need to cover those points using other lines, each line can cover at most one point in that column. So we need at least n+1-a lines that intersect that column (i.e., lines that are not vertical). So for each column without a vertical line, we need at least n+1-a lines among the remaining lines.\n\nThus, if we have v vertical lines, we need to ensure that for each column not covered by a vertical line, we have enough non-vertical lines to cover all points in that column. Since we have total n lines, v vertical lines, and n-v non-vertical lines. So the maximum number of points we can cover in any column without a vertical line is n-v (since each non-vertical line can cover at most one point in that column). So we need n+1-a ≤ n-v for each column a not covered by a vertical line. That is, v ≤ a-1. Wait, rearrange: n+1-a ≤ n-v => cancel n: 1-a ≤ -v => v ≤ a-1. So for any column a that is not covered by a vertical line, we must have v ≤ a-1. That is, the number of vertical lines must be at most a-1. But v is a global number, not dependent on a. So for the smallest a among columns not covered by vertical lines, we need v ≤ a-1. So if we have v vertical lines, the columns not covered must be those with a > v (since v ≤ a-1 => a ≥ v+1). So the columns that can be left uncovered by vertical lines are those with index at least v+1. So we can only leave uncovered columns from v+1 to n. So we can have at most n-v columns uncovered, which is consistent.\n\nThus, the condition is that we can choose v vertical lines covering columns 1,...,v (or any v columns, but we can reorder). Then the remaining columns are v+1,...,n. For each such column a, we need to cover n+1-a points using the n-v non-vertical lines. Since n-v lines can each cover at most one point per column, we need n+1-a ≤ n-v => a ≤ v+1? Wait, we derived earlier v ≤ a-1. Let's re-derive carefully.\n\nSuppose we have v vertical lines covering some set of columns. For any column a not covered by a vertical line, we have n-v non-vertical lines. Each non-vertical line can cover at most one point in column a. So the maximum number of points we can cover in column a is n-v. But the column a has n+1-a points. So we need n+1-a ≤ n-v => subtract n: 1-a ≤ -v => v ≤ a-1. So indeed v ≤ a-1. So for any uncovered column a, we must have v ≤ a-1. So the smallest uncovered column must satisfy a ≥ v+1. So the uncovered columns must be at least v+1. So we can leave uncovered columns from v+1 onward. So the condition is consistent: we can cover columns 1,...,v with vertical lines, and columns v+1,...,n must be covered by non-vertical lines.\n\nNow, for column a = v+1, we have n+1-(v+1) = n-v points. So we need exactly n-v non-vertical lines to cover all points in column v+1. Since we have exactly n-v non-vertical lines, each must cover exactly one point in column v+1. So each non-vertical line must intersect column v+1 at a distinct point. So each non-vertical line must pass through a distinct point in column v+1. So the set of points in column v+1 must be exactly the intersection points of the non-vertical lines with that column.\n\nNow, for column a = v+2, we have n+1-(v+2) = n-v-1 points. We have n-v non-vertical lines, but each can cover at most one point in column v+2. So we have one extra line that must not intersect column v+2 (or intersect at a point already covered? Actually each line can intersect column v+2 at at most one point, but we have more lines than points, so some lines will not intersect column v+2 at any point (i.e., they are parallel to column v+2? Actually a line can intersect column v+2 at some point if it's not vertical. But we can choose lines that intersect column v+2 at points that are already covered? No, each line can intersect column v+2 at at most one point, but we have n-v lines and n-v-1 points to cover, so at least one line will not intersect column v+2 at any point (i.e., it will be parallel to column v+2? But column v+2 is a vertical line x=v+2. A non-vertical line always intersects any vertical line at exactly one point (unless it's parallel to vertical line, which would be vertical itself). So any non-vertical line will intersect column v+2 at exactly one point. So each non-vertical line will intersect column v+2 at some point. But we only need to cover n-v-1 points. So some points will be covered multiple times (i.e., some lines intersect column v+2 at points that are not in the set of points we need to cover? Actually the column v+2 has points (v+2,b) for b=1,...,n+1-(v+2) = n-v-1. So there are exactly n-v-1 points. If we have n-v non-vertical lines, each line will intersect column v+2 at some point (v+2,b_i). Since there are only n-v-1 distinct points, by pigeonhole principle at least two lines will intersect column v+2 at the same point, i.e., they will both pass through the same point (v+2,b). That is allowed (multiple lines can pass through same point). So we can have overlapping coverage. So it's not a problem.\n\nThus the condition for covering all points is that we can choose v vertical lines covering columns 1,...,v, and then we need to cover the remaining columns with n-v non-vertical lines. The non-vertical lines must be arranged such that they intersect column v+1 at all n-v points (i.e., each point in column v+1 must be on a distinct non-vertical line). Then for each subsequent column, the lines intersect at points, possibly overlapping.\n\nNow, the classification of non-vertical lines: they can be horizontal, diagonal, or sunny. The number of sunny lines is k. So among the n-v non-vertical lines, we have k sunny lines, and the rest (n-v-k) are either horizontal or diagonal.\n\nThus we need to see if we can have k≥4 while satisfyin