ne point per column. Equivalent to constructing a set of 4 lines that pass through these points. Let's try to find such lines.\n\nWe need to assign each point to a line such that each line passes through points from different columns (maybe multiple columns). Since each line can intersect each column at most once, but can intersect multiple columns (i.e., can pass through points from multiple columns). So we need to partition the points into at most 4 groups, each group being collinear (with a line not vertical/horizontal/slope -1). Let's try to find such grouping.\n\nWe have 10 points. With 4 lines, average group size is 2.5. So some lines will have 2 or 3 points. Let's try to find lines that cover multiple points across columns.\n\nPotential lines:\n\n- L1: through (1,1) and (2,2) slope = (1-2)/(1-2) = 1, allowed. This line also passes through (3,3) which is already covered by L0 (non-sunny). But that's okay; we can have overlapping coverage. However, (3,3) is already covered, but we can still use L1 to cover (1,1),(2,2). It also passes through (4,4) not in region. So L1 covers (1,1),(2,2). Good.\n\n- L2: through (1,2) and (3,1) slope = (2-1)/(1-3) = 1/(-2) = -0.5, allowed. This line also passes through (2,1.5) not integer. So L2 covers (1,2),(3,1). Good.\n\n- L3: through (1,3) and (4,1) slope = (3-1)/(1-4) = 2/(-3) = -2/3, allowed. This line also passes through (2,2.333...), not integer. So L3 covers (1,3),(4,1). Good.\n\n- L4: through (1,4) and (2,3) slope = (4-3)/(1-2) = 1/(-1) = -1, not allowed (slope -1). So can't use that. Maybe use (1,4) and (3,2) slope = (4-2)/(1-3) = 2/(-2) = -1, also not allowed. (1,4) and (2,1) slope = (4-1)/(1-2) = 3/(-1) = -3, allowed. So L4: through (1,4) and (2,1) slope -3. This line also passes through (3,-2) not needed. So L4 covers (1,4),(2,1). Good.\n\nNow we have covered points:\n\n- L1: (1,1),(2,2)\n- L2: (1,2),(3,1)\n- L3: (1,3),(4,1)\n- L4: (1,4),(2,1)\n\nRemaining points: (2,3) (not covered), (3,2) (not covered). Also (2,3)